{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "בננו", :translit "bninu", :gloss "our son"} {:he "חסר יו\"ד", :translit "chaser yud", :gloss "deficient yod"} {:he "עשרת הדברות", :translit "aseret ha-dibrot", :gloss "the Ten Commandments"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "בננו", :b "עשרת הדברות"}], :frame {:text "The word בננו in Deuteronomy 21:20 is written defectively, missing the letter yud. Ba'al HaTurim notes that the gematria of בננו (defective) equals עשרת הדברות, hinting that the rebellious son has transgressed all Ten Commandments. The Talmud Sanhedrin 70a records the view that the rebellious son is punished for his ultimate trajectory—he would eventually exhaust his father's resources and turn to robbery, violating all of God's commandments.", :sources ["Sanhedrin 70a" "Deuteronomy 21:20"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ומרה", :translit "u-merah", :gloss "and rebellious"} {:he "חסר וי\"ו", :translit "chaser vav", :gloss "deficient vav"} {:he "מרה יהיה לו באחרונה", :translit "marah yihyeh lo ba-acharonah", :gloss "bitter he will be to him in the end"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "ומרה", :b "מרה יהיה לו באחרונה"}], :frame {:text "The word ומרה in Deuteronomy 21:20 is written defectively, missing the letter vav. Ba'al HaTurim reads this as hinting that the gematria of ומרה (defective) equals מרה יהיה לו באחרונה, 'he will ultimately be bitter to him.' This reflects the talmudic principle at Sanhedrin 72a that the rebellious son is judged for what he will become—his end (sof) rather than his present state—since if left unchecked he would bring bitter grief upon his father.", :sources ["Sanhedrin 72a" "Deuteronomy 21:20"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "בננו", :a-value 108, :b "עשרת הדברות", :b-value 1587, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "ומרה", :a-value 251, :b "מרה יהיה לו באחרונה", :b-value 583, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :discipli